Intel", "lp": -0.47424396872520447, "top": [[" Intel", -0.47424396872520447], [" sources", -1.0672138929367065]]}, {"t": " specs", "lp": -1.6701693534851074, "top": [[" sources", -1.2132678031921387], [" specs", -1.6701693534851074]]}, {"t": ".", "lp": -0.03558057174086571, "top": [[".", -0.03558057174086571], [" and", -4.008875846862793]]}, {"t": " The", "lp": -0.481220006942749, "top": [[" The", -0.481220006942749], [" If", -1.0959948301315308]]}, {"t": " CCS", "lp": -1.79500412940979, "top": [[" CCS", -1.79500412940979], [" modes", -2.1206648349761963]]}, {"t": " mode", "lp": -0.1682397425174713, "top": [[" mode", -0.1682397425174713], [" modes", -2.0660650730133057]]}, {"t": " parameter", "lp": -0.8415544033050537, "top": [[" parameter", -0.8415544033050537], [" behavior", -1.1824606657028198]]}, {"t": " has", "lp": -1.1965998411178589, "top": [[" is", -0.5857905745506287], [" has", -1.1965998411178589]]}, {"t": " evolved", "lp": -0.5842978954315186, "top": [[" evolved", -0.5842978954315186], [" been", -1.6359660625457764]]}], "text": "# Xe CCS Mode on Intel Arc GPUs\n\nThe `XeCcsMode` parameter controls how the **compute CCS (Command Streamer)** is set up when the Xe driver initializes. This affects how GPU compute workloads are dispatched.\n\n## The Three Modes\n\n**Mode 1 (default):**\n- Uses a single dedicated CCS for compute operations\n- Simpler setup with one command streamer handling compute\n- Good default behavior for most workloads\n\n**Mode 2:**\n- Uses multiple CCS instances (one per GPU slice/subslice group)\n- Allows more parallel command processing across the GPU's MUs\n- Can improve performance for heavy compute workloads that benefit from parallel dispatch\n\n**Mode 4:**\n- Uses a specific CCS configuration suited for particular workloads\n\n## When to Use Each\n\nThe practical guidance is:\n\n- **Mode 1** \u2014 Default choice; works well for most use cases including gaming and general compute\n- **Mode 2** \u2014 Consider for heavy GPU compute workloads (machine learning, rendering, scientific computing) where parallel dispatch can help throughput\n- **Mode 4** \u2014 Historically associated with specific optimization scenarios\n\n## Practical Reality\n\nHonestly, the differences are often **subtle** and workload-dependent. In many real-world scenarios:\n\n- For **gaming**: Mode 1 is typically fine\n- For **compute**: Mode 2 or 4 may offer improvements, but results vary\n\n## How to Set It\n\nYou can set it via kernel module parameter:\n\n```bash\n# /etc/modprobe.d/xe.conf\noptions xe ccs_mode=2\n```\n\nThen reload the driver or reboot.\n\n---\n\n**A caveat:** I'm not fully certain about the precise technical distinction of mode 4 versus the others, as these details aren't extensively documented in public Intel specs. The CCS mode parameter has evolved"}